---
/**
 * NetWorthEducationalContent.astro
 * Educational guide explaining net worth calculations, asset & liability categorization,
 * valuation principles, balance sheet limitations, and tracking best practices.
 */
---

<div class="nec-wrapper" id="how-net-worth-calculations-work">
  <!-- ─── Section Header ─── -->
  <header class="nec-header">
    <span class="nec-eyebrow font-mono">Educational Guide</span>
    <h2 class="nec-main-title">How Net Worth Calculations Work</h2>
    <p class="nec-lead">
      Your net worth is a fundamental personal finance metric that summarizes your financial position
      at a single point in time by comparing everything you own against everything you owe.
    </p>
  </header>

  <!-- ─── 1. Basic Net Worth Formula ─── -->
  <section class="nec-section">
    <h3 class="nec-section-title">1. The Net Worth Formula</h3>
    <div class="nec-prose">
      <p>
        Calculating net worth follows a simple, universal accounting equation:
      </p>

      <div class="nec-formula-card">
        <span class="nec-formula-badge font-mono">Balance Sheet Equation</span>
        <div class="nec-formula-math font-mono">
          Net Worth = Total Assets &minus; Total Liabilities
        </div>
        <p class="nec-formula-caption">
          <strong>Assets</strong> are items of positive financial value that you own. <strong>Liabilities</strong> are financial debts and obligations that you owe to others.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        Net worth provides a clear snapshot of your accumulated wealth at the moment of calculation.
        A positive net worth means your assets exceed your debts, while a negative net worth occurs when
        total liabilities exceed total assets (common early in careers or after taking on student or mortgage debt).
        For a detailed walkthrough on personal balance sheet tracking, explore our guide to <a href="/blog/net-worth-basics">net worth basics</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 2. Understanding Assets ─── -->
  <section class="nec-section">
    <h3 class="nec-section-title">2. Assets: What You Own</h3>
    <div class="nec-prose">
      <p>
        Assets represent resources with measurable monetary value. When estimating current net worth,
        assets should generally be entered using their <strong>estimated current market value</strong> (what
        they could sell for today) rather than their original purchase price.
      </p>
      <p>
        The CalcVantage Net Worth Calculator groups assets into seven core categories:
      </p>
      <ul class="nec-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Cash &amp; Savings:</strong> Liquid funds in checking accounts, high-yield savings accounts, money market funds, and physical cash.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Investments:</strong> Taxable brokerage accounts, equities, bonds, mutual funds, exchange-traded funds (ETFs), and cryptocurrencies.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Retirement Accounts:</strong> Dedicated retirement savings vehicles such as 401(k)s, Traditional or Roth IRAs, Canadian RRSPs/TFSAs, and Australian Superannuation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Real Estate:</strong> The estimated fair market value of primary residences, vacation homes, and investment or rental properties.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Vehicles:</strong> The estimated resale value of cars, motorcycles, boats, and recreational vehicles.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Business Assets:</strong> Equity value, capital equipment, inventory, or ownership stakes in privately held businesses.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Other Assets:</strong> Tangible valuables such as collectibles, fine art, jewelry, tools, and other personal property of significant resale value.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 3. Understanding Liabilities ─── -->
  <section class="nec-section">
    <h3 class="nec-section-title">3. Liabilities: What You Owe</h3>
    <div class="nec-prose">
      <p>
        Liabilities encompass all outstanding debts and contractual obligations owed to banks, lenders,
        or institutions. Every dollar of debt directly reduces your net worth.
      </p>
      <p>
        The calculator tracks six distinct liability categories:
      </p>
      <ul class="nec-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Mortgage Debt:</strong> The remaining principal balance owed on home loans and investment property mortgages.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Personal Loans:</strong> Unsecured bank loans, peer-to-peer loans, and personal lines of credit.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Auto Loans:</strong> Outstanding balances on vehicle financing, leases, and auto debt.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Credit Card Debt:</strong> Revolving balances carried on consumer credit cards and store cards.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Student Loans:</strong> Government and private education loans and tuition debt.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Other Liabilities:</strong> Back taxes owed, medical bills, legal judgments, and other personal financial obligations.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="nec-table-card">
        <div class="nec-table-header">
          <h4 class="nec-table-title font-mono">Understanding Assets and Liabilities</h4>
        </div>
        <div class="nec-table-wrap">
          <table class="nec-data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Classification</th>
                <th>Category</th>
                <th>Examples / What It Includes</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="7" class="nec-type-cell text-asset"><strong>Assets</strong><br /><span class="nec-type-sub">(What you own)</span></td>
                <td><strong>Cash &amp; Savings</strong></td>
                <td>Checking, high-yield savings, money market funds, cash</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Investments</strong></td>
                <td>Taxable brokerage accounts, stocks, bonds, ETFs, crypto</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Retirement Accounts</strong></td>
                <td>401(k), IRA, Roth IRA, RRSP, TFSA, Superannuation</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Real Estate</strong></td>
                <td>Primary residence, vacation property, rental real estate</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Vehicles</strong></td>
                <td>Estimated fair market value of cars, motorcycles, boats</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Business Assets</strong></td>
                <td>Business equity, machinery, inventory, ownership stakes</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Other Assets</strong></td>
                <td>Valuables, collectibles, jewelry, tools, personal property</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="table-divider-row">
                <td rowspan="6" class="nec-type-cell text-liability"><strong>Liabilities</strong><br /><span class="nec-type-sub">(What you owe)</span></td>
                <td><strong>Mortgage</strong></td>
                <td>Remaining principal on home and investment property loans</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Personal Loans</strong></td>
                <td>Unsecured personal loans, lines of credit</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Auto Loans</strong></td>
                <td>Outstanding vehicle financing and auto lease balances</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Credit Card Debt</strong></td>
                <td>Revolving balances carried on consumer credit cards</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Student Loans</strong></td>
                <td>Education loans, tuition balances, student debt</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Other Liabilities</strong></td>
                <td>Taxes owed, medical debt, informal obligations</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 4. Categorization Logic & Leveraged Assets ─── -->
  <section class="nec-section">
    <h3 class="nec-section-title">4. Categorizing Leveraged Assets</h3>
    <div class="nec-prose">
      <p>
        A common point of confusion when building a personal balance sheet is how to record financed items,
        such as a mortgaged home or a financed car.
      </p>
      <p>
        To maintain accounting accuracy, the asset and the debt are separated into their respective categories:
      </p>
      <div class="nec-example-card">
        <div class="nec-example-header">
          <span class="nec-example-tag font-mono">Example: Property with a Mortgage</span>
        </div>
        <div class="nec-concept-grid">
          <div class="nec-concept-col">
            <span class="nec-concept-type text-asset font-mono">Asset Side</span>
            <span class="nec-concept-name">Real Estate</span>
            <span class="nec-concept-val font-mono">$500,000</span>
            <p class="nec-concept-desc">Full current market value of the home.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="nec-concept-divider font-mono">&minus;</div>
          <div class="nec-concept-col">
            <span class="nec-concept-type text-liability font-mono">Liability Side</span>
            <span class="nec-concept-name">Mortgage Loan</span>
            <span class="nec-concept-val font-mono">$350,000</span>
            <p class="nec-concept-desc">Remaining debt owed to the lender.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="nec-concept-divider font-mono">=</div>
          <div class="nec-concept-col highlight">
            <span class="nec-concept-type font-mono">Net Contribution</span>
            <span class="nec-concept-name">Home Equity</span>
            <span class="nec-concept-val font-mono text-net">$150,000</span>
            <p class="nec-concept-desc">Your true equity contributing to net worth.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="nec-caption">
        Entering both the total asset value and the total outstanding liability ensures that your balance sheet reflects both gross asset allocation and debt obligations accurately.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 5. Illustrative Net Worth Example ─── -->
  <section class="nec-section">
    <h3 class="nec-section-title">5. An Illustrative Net Worth Calculation</h3>
    <div class="nec-prose">
      <p>
        Consider a simple, hypothetical balance sheet breakdown:
      </p>

      <div class="nec-example-card">
        <div class="nec-example-header">
          <span class="nec-example-tag font-mono">Illustrative Example</span>
          <span class="nec-example-note font-mono">Sample personal balance sheet</span>
        </div>
        <div class="nec-timeline-table-wrap">
          <table class="nec-timeline-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Category</th>
                <th>Classification</th>
                <th>Amount</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Cash, Investments, &amp; Retirement</td>
                <td class="font-mono text-asset">Asset</td>
                <td class="font-mono">$100,000</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Real Estate (Market Value)</td>
                <td class="font-mono text-asset">Asset</td>
                <td class="font-mono">$375,000</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Vehicles &amp; Other Personal Property</td>
                <td class="font-mono text-asset">Asset</td>
                <td class="font-mono">$25,000</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-total">
                <td><strong>Total Assets</strong></td>
                <td class="font-mono text-asset"><strong>Total</strong></td>
                <td class="font-mono text-asset"><strong>$500,000</strong></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Mortgage Balance</td>
                <td class="font-mono text-liability">Liability</td>
                <td class="font-mono">$170,000</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Auto Loan &amp; Student Debt</td>
                <td class="font-mono text-liability">Liability</td>
                <td class="font-mono">$30,000</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-total">
                <td><strong>Total Liabilities</strong></td>
                <td class="font-mono text-liability"><strong>Total</strong></td>
                <td class="font-mono text-liability"><strong>$200,000</strong></td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-networth">
                <td><strong>Calculated Net Worth</strong></td>
                <td class="font-mono"><strong>Result</strong></td>
                <td class="font-mono text-net"><strong>$300,000</strong></td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="nec-caption">
        This illustrative balance sheet demonstrates how $500,000 in total assets minus $200,000 in total liabilities yields a net worth of $300,000.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 6. What Net Worth Does & Does Not Tell You ─── -->
  <section class="nec-section">
    <h3 class="nec-section-title">6. What Net Worth Does &amp; Does Not Measure</h3>
    <div class="nec-prose">
      <p>
        While net worth is one of the most comprehensive single measures of personal wealth, it is important
        to recognize its practical limitations:
      </p>
      <ul class="nec-list">
        <li>
          <strong>It does not measure income or cash flow:</strong> A person with high net worth tied up in real estate can still experience tight monthly cash flow if their liquid income is low.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It does not reflect liquidity:</strong> Net worth treats illiquid physical assets (like vehicles or property) identically to cash, even though selling physical assets takes time and incurs transaction fees.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It does not measure emergency readiness:</strong> Having $200,000 in retirement accounts does not replace having an accessible liquid emergency fund in cash.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 7. Updating & Tracking Over Time ─── -->
  <section class="nec-section">
    <h3 class="nec-section-title">7. Tracking Changes Over Time</h3>
    <div class="nec-prose">
      <p>
        Net worth is dynamic and changes continuously as financial actions occur:
      </p>
      <ul class="nec-list">
        <li><strong>Debt Reduction:</strong> Every principal payment made toward a mortgage, student loan, or credit card directly reduces liabilities and increases net worth.</li>
        <li><strong>Asset Growth &amp; Savings:</strong> Adding new money to savings, retirement accounts, or investment portfolios increases total assets.</li>
        <li><strong>Market Fluctuations:</strong> Market movements in stock portfolios and home value changes will cause net worth to fluctuate independently of your savings rate.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Updating your figures periodically (such as quarterly or semi-annually) allows you to track your long-term wealth trajectory and verify that debt is trending down while assets trend upward.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 8. Standardized Disclaimer ─── -->
  <div class="nec-disclaimer" role="note">
    <span class="nec-disclaimer-title font-mono">Disclaimer &amp; Terms of Use</span>
    <p>
      CalcVantage calculators provide estimates for educational and informational purposes only.
      Results depend entirely on the accuracy and completeness of the figures entered by the user.
      Calculations do not constitute formal accounting, tax, legal, or financial advice.
      For formal estate planning, tax preparation, or wealth management, consult with a certified public
      accountant (CPA) or licensed financial planner.
      For full terms, please review our <a href="/disclaimer">Disclaimer</a> and <a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /* ── Wrapper ── */
  .nec-wrapper {
    padding: 3rem 0 2rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 2.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }

  /* ── Header ── */
  .nec-header {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.625rem;
    max-width: 48rem;
  }

  .nec-eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-mute);
  }

  .nec-main-title {
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vw, 2.25rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .nec-lead {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-body);
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* ── Sections & Prose ── */
  .nec-section {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.875rem;
  }

  .nec-section-title {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .nec-prose {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.875rem;
    max-width: 54rem;
  }

  .nec-prose p {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-body);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .nec-caption {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-mute);
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin-top: -0.25rem;
  }

  /* ── Lists ── */
  .nec-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0.25rem 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.625rem;
  }

  .nec-list li {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-body);
    padding-left: 1rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-hairline-strong);
  }

  .nec-list li strong {
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* ── Table Cards & Data Tables ── */
  .nec-table-card {
    background-color: var(--color-canvas);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
    overflow: hidden;
    margin: 0.75rem 0;
  }

  .nec-table-header {
    padding: 0.875rem 1.25rem;
    background-color: var(--color-canvas-soft);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
  }

  .nec-table-title {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .nec-table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .nec-data-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    text-align: left;
  }

  .nec-data-table th {
    padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem;
    background-color: var(--color-canvas-soft-2);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .nec-data-table td {
    padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    color: var(--color-body);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .nec-data-table tbody tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  .nec-data-table tbody tr.table-divider-row td {
    border-top: 2px solid var(--color-hairline-strong);
  }

  .nec-type-cell {
    background-color: var(--color-canvas-soft);
    vertical-align: top;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .nec-type-sub {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--color-mute);
    font-weight: normal;
  }

  /* ── Formula Cards ── */
  .nec-formula-card {
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    background-color: var(--color-canvas);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin: 0.5rem 0;
  }

  .nec-formula-badge {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-mute);
  }

  .nec-formula-math {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    padding: 0.5rem 0;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .nec-formula-caption {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-mute);
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* ── Example Cards & Tables ── */
  .nec-example-card {
    background-color: var(--color-canvas);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
    overflow: hidden;
    margin: 0.5rem 0;
  }

  .nec-example-header {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding: 0.875rem 1.25rem;
    background-color: var(--color-canvas-soft);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
  }

  .nec-example-tag {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .nec-example-note {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--color-mute);
  }

  /* ── Concept Grid (Home Equity) ── */
  .nec-concept-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    align-items: center;
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .nec-concept-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr auto 1.2fr;
    }
  }

  .nec-concept-col {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.25rem;
    padding: 0.75rem;
    background-color: var(--color-canvas-soft-2);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  .nec-concept-col.highlight {
    background-color: var(--color-accent-soft);
  }

  .nec-concept-type {
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .nec-concept-name {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .nec-concept-val {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .nec-concept-desc {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--color-body);
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

